<p>Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian released an open letter to the American public that mixed criticism of the United States&#x27; role in the war with Iran with language that the New York Times said suggested diplomatic engagement was possible. The letter appeared hours before President Trump was due to address Americans about the war. In the message, Pezeshkian said ordinary Americans are not Iran’s enemy and argued that portraying Iran as a threat does not match historical reality or current facts. Across the coverage, the clearest element was the combination of confrontation and outreach: Pezeshkian criticized what he called a costly and futile path of conflict, while the New York Times said the letter also pointed to possible diplomatic engagement. His appeal centered on urging Americans to question whether the Middle East war fits the “America First” priorities of their government.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/kAHQIwARgKZTuRCi1oYBIah3TL3SEZlZ879faoZ79b_-T-wUmVCjhZhAk2m4QLzfQ0GMlZozKHf5rW83Lzdz9MdoS6qNEUMUMvmIcYW8ykDbqk-Nxm3hdzOeNkEoQnLDM3fINry5pGGvMCJ8HiH-c7CQAvOhjKaf7fr-uT8TQ3nzkZxbApwslko2KdT_fbbYnNazt3_OYDq46wo_JwQ234w' alt='President Masoud Pezeshkian of Iran in Tehran in February.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Direct appeal: Pezeshkian addressed not only Americans but also people seeking “truth” amid what he described as distortions and manufactured narratives, according to the published text of the letter.</li><li>Historical framing: In the letter, Pezeshkian said Iran had not chosen aggression, expansion, colonialism or domination in its modern history and had instead repelled attacks against it.</li><li>Historical warning: He contrasted Iran’s endurance with the fate of past aggressors, saying they were left as “tarnished names in history,” according to the South China Morning Post’s report on the letter.</li></ul><blockquote>Continuing along the path of confrontation is more costly and futile than ever before. - President Masoud Pezeshkian</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Masoud Pezeshkian: He told Americans that Iranians distinguish between governments and the peoples they govern, presenting that as a deeply rooted cultural principle rather than a temporary political line. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987606/read-the-full-text-of-iranian-president-masoud-pezeshkians-letter-to-the-american-public'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>The New York Times: The paper characterized the letter’s tone as alternating between defiant, patronizing and conciliatory while noting that it also pointed to possible diplomatic engagement. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/01/world/middleeast/iran-president-pezeshkian-letter-diplomacy.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Straits Times coverage: The outlet emphasized the text of the appeal itself by publishing both a news report on the message and the full letter. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-president-asks-us-people-if-mid-east-war-puts-america-first'>The Straits Times</a>, <a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/full-text-of-iranian-president-masoud-pezeshkians-letter-to-americans'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/01/world/middleeast/iran-president-pezeshkian-letter-diplomacy.html'>Iran’s President Suggests Diplomatic Engagement Possible in Letter to U.S. Public</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/full-text-of-iranian-president-masoud-pezeshkians-letter-to-americans'>Full text of Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian’s letter to Americans</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iranian-president-says-in-letter-that-iran-harbors-no-enmity-towards-ordinary-americans/article70814039.ece'>Iranian President says in letter that Iran harbors no enmity towards ordinary Americans</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/arti